# SYNOPSIS

```swift
enum ValhallBackend {
  static func compile(
    spirv: [UInt32], entryPoint: String?,
    specialization: [(id: UInt32, bits: UInt64)],
    options: BackendOptions) throws(BackendError) -> CompiledShader
  static func compile(
    _ module: IRModule,
    options: BackendOptions) throws(BackendError) -> CompiledShader
}
struct CompiledShader {
  var shader: ComputeShader
  var layout: FauLayout
  var instructions: [Instruction]
}
extension ValhallBackend {
  static func compileGraphics(
    spirv: [UInt32], entryPoint: String?,
    specialization: [(id: UInt32, bits: UInt64)],
    options: BackendOptions) throws(BackendError) -> CompiledGraphicsShader
  static func compileGraphics(
    _ module: IRModule,
    options: BackendOptions) throws(BackendError) -> CompiledGraphicsShader
}
struct CompiledGraphicsShader {
  var vertex: VertexShader?
  var fragment: FragmentShader?
  var instructions: [Instruction]
}
```

# DESCRIPTION

The backend turns a compute shader into the words MaliCore's dispatch runs, and a vertex or a fragment shader into the words its draw runs.
It runs on the device, when a pipeline is made, and on the host, in tests.
It is Swift, tier 0.

The work, in order: calls are inlined, the IR's passes run and scalarise, instructions are selected over virtual registers, FAU and constant sources are fitted to the instruction limits, registers are allocated, message slots and waits are placed, `reconverge` and `end` are added, the branches are resolved, and the ISA validator checks the result (a broken rule is `invalidOutput`).
The output `ComputeShader` holds the code, the register class (32 when every register is in r0 to r15 or r48 to r63), the preload mask, the workgroup size, the stack and workgroup memory bytes, the FAU words, whether workgroup merging is allowed and the flush mode.

## The ABI

Preloads are read in place: global invocation id in r60 to r62 (mask bits 12 to 14), workgroup id in r57 to r59, local invocation id in r55 and r56 (unpacked at the start).
The lane id is the special FAU `lane_id`; a shader that reads it, uses workgroup memory or a barrier does not allow merging.
The dispatch base is not applied: the run state's offset is zero.
Workgroup memory is `workgroup_local_pointer` plus a byte offset; stack, private and function variables and spills are the thread's stack, reached at `thread_local_pointer` with access `force`.

## Vertex and fragment shaders

`compileGraphics` compiles the first vertex or fragment entry point (or the one named) the same way, into the data MaliCore builds a draw from: a `VertexShader` or a `FragmentShader` (code, register class: 32 when its values fit r0 to r15 and r48 to r63, which doubles the threads a core keeps resident, else 64; preload mask, `GraphicsFauLayout`, and the locations the shader reads and writes).
The two stages and the driver agree on the ABI below, all of it in `GraphicsShader.swift`.
In these stages, after the calls are inlined, the private variables become the function's own (`localizePrivateGlobals`, shadercompiler(2)), so a vertex input the Vulkan lowering made a private variable is a value, not stack memory; after the IR's passes, each load of memory nothing writes moves to its use (`sinkLoadsToUses`), and two to four 32-bit loads of global memory at consecutive offsets from one base, in one block and at most 24 instructions apart, are one `LOAD.i64`, `i96` or `i128` (unaligned global accesses are the hardware's, valhall 12a.1.6), each word read from its register of the message's.
A uniform block's matrix column, or a vertex input's components, is then one message.

A vertex shader is one program for both IDVS passes, which stores two groups of outputs, each behind a test of `shader_output`: the position (bit 0) and the generic varyings (bit 2).
A shader of one block with generic varyings, one view, no memory writes and no NoPerspective varying is divided between the passes: what both groups need runs first, then the instructions only the position needs with its stores behind bit 0, then those only the varyings need with theirs behind bit 2, so the position pass does none of the varyings' work and the varying pass none of the position's (`shader_output` is read once; a shader where fewer than eight instructions are one group's alone is not divided).
Any other runs whole in each pass and ends in the two groups.
Preloads are r59 (the vertex's slot in the hardware vertex buffer, always), r60 (the vertex index) and r61 (the instance index); an attribute (a Location input) is read with `LD_ATTR_IMM` from table 0 at the index of its location, taking r60 and r61.
The position is divided by w and put through the viewport transform in the shader (valhall 14.3.3): `r` is the reciprocal of w clamped to a magnitude of 2^15, `x * r * scale + offset` for x, y and z, and r is the stored w; the scale and offset are FAU words.
The record is stored with `STORE.i128.istream` at the address `LEA_BUF_IMM` gives from table 61, buffer 0, and r59.
A varying is stored with `STORE.estream` through table 61, buffer 1, at 16 bytes times its location, so the fragment shader finds it knowing nothing of the vertex shader, and `varyingBytes` is the size to give the draw.
A varying the fragment shader declares NoPerspective is stored times the position's w, chosen at compile time by `BackendOptions.nonPerspectiveMask` (the fragment shader's `nonPerspectiveMask`), or at run time by a bit of a FAU word when the option is nil.
PointSize is accepted and not stored, so the position record keeps its basic format.

A fragment shader's preloads are r60 (the coverage, always), r59 (the pixel position: FragCoord's x and y are those plus a half), r58 (the facing) and r61 (centroid inputs).
A Location input is read with `LD_VAR_BUF_IMM` at 16 bytes times its location (flat for an integer or Flat input), once, at the start; FragCoord's z and w and a NoPerspective input's multiplier are `LD_VAR_SPECIAL`.
The first interpolating load computes the weights into the hidden register (`store`) and a later one at the same sample position reuses them (`retrieve`, 12c.2.4); a retrieving load waits for the one that stored, no other (12c.5.5), and a load after a `frag_w` stores again.
OpKill is `DISCARD`, and the path goes to the end.
Every path ends in the epilogue of 12c.6.4: a wait, `ATEST` (the alpha of colour 0, or 1), a wait, and one `BLEND` for each colour output, to the render target of its location and the special blend descriptor of the same number; the last carries `end`.

With `BackendOptions.blendShaders` a fragment shader may call a blend shader (valhall 09.9): each `BLEND` has its colour copied to r0 to r3 (a dual-source shader's second output to r4 to r7), is followed by the 16-byte call sequence (`ADR r48` from the program counter plus 8, or zero for the last, which has `end` on the `BLEND`, then `JUMP` through the descriptor's address) and skips it by 16 bytes when the target is not in mode Shader; the colours of other targets live outside r0 to r15 and r48; the first four FAU words are the blend constants and the push constants follow.
`compileBlendShader(spec)` makes the shader for a `BlendShaderSpec`: straight-line code in r0 to r15 and r48 that loads the destination (`LD_TILE`, f16), does the equation, mask or logic operation in f32, and stores with an opaque `BLEND`; the validator checks it as a blend shader.
It does 8-bit unorm and sRGB targets, on a source of f32; the factors are all fifteen and the four of the second source, the operations all five, and a logic operation works on the 8-bit codes.

The vertex shader's FAU block is the viewport (three slots: scale and offset of x, then y, then z), then the non-perspective mask when read, then the push constants; the fragment shader's is the push constants.
`GraphicsFauLayout.block` builds it.
A buffer bound by descriptor is refused in these stages: the binding is the root pointer (a pointer in the push constants, read as a global pointer).

## Textures and samplers

The shader names images and samplers by (set, binding) and an element index, and the descriptor model of the driver is one bindless heap: a table of 32-byte descriptors, textures and samplers by index, which is table 1 of the resource table (table 0 is the driver's).
For each binding a fragment shader names, the driver chooses the heap index of element 0 and writes its handle (`table << 24 | index`, `DescriptorHeap.handle(index:)`) in a FAU word.
The words are the first of the fragment block, in ascending (set, binding) order (`GraphicsFauLayout.imageBindings`), and a word for the dummy sampler follows them when a fetch names no sampler (`dummySamplerWord`); the push constants follow.
`GraphicsFauLayout.block(images:dummySampler:)` fills them.

A texture access is one message, or two (gradients).
A compute shader's texture access is the same message, with the heap's handles for its bindings in its FAU block as a fragment shader's are (and the dummy sampler word, for a fetch of an image with no sampler); it names its level (no derivatives outside the fragment stage).
`TEX_SINGLE` serves a sample, with the level mode `computed` (an implicit level, from the quad's derivatives), `computed_bias` (the level word is the bias), `explicit` (the level word), `none` (an explicit level of zero, no word) or `grdesc`, and `TEX_FETCH` a fetch, whose level and (for a multisampled image) sample are bytes of its offset word.
A sample with explicit gradients is a `TEX_GRADIENT` of the derivatives (interleaved per axis, with the sampler's bias and limits left to the sample) whose two result words go to the last two words of the `TEX_SINGLE.grdesc`'s staging block.
A gather is `TEX_GATHER` (any component, an offset word, a depth reference; four offsets are four gathers), whose channels the backend does not permute (the lowering reads them in the API's order).
A depth compare is `compare` on `TEX_SINGLE` or `TEX_GATHER`.
A cube image is read in the direction form (`project`, three coordinate words), 12b.5.3.
OpImageQueryLod is a `TEX_GRADIENT` in coordinate mode with the clamp disabled, and the rest is arithmetic over the sampler's descriptor words.
The handle source is the wide form, a register pair: word 0 the sampler's handle, word 1 the texture's, each the binding's FAU word plus the element index, computed in the shader.
A handle is one value for a warp, and a fragment warp mixes primitives and instances, so an element index that is not the same in every lane (a flat varying, an instance or vertex index, a position: anything but constants, push constants and loads at addresses every lane has) is made uniform by a loop over the distinct values: the lowest waiting lane's index is broadcast (`WMASK`, `CLPER`), the lanes that have it do the access and are done, and the loop ends when no lane waits (at most sixteen rounds); a `discard` runs after it.
A fetch of a plain image (OpImage of a sampled image drops the sampler) names the dummy sampler.
The staging block is one register for each operand in the order of valhall 12b.5: the coordinates, the layer, the offset word, the level word.
The wide form has no narrow word, so a constant offset, the array index and a fetch's level (zero needs none) are staged.
The handle pair of a texture and sampler is made once a block, and so is a constant that needs a register of its own (a filter's taps share both).
The result block holds the channels the shader reads, in order, 32 bits each, and its register type field (`f`, `u` or `s`) is the texel's kind.
`skip` is clear, since an implicit level needs the helper lanes' coordinates, and the shader has no `discard` flow, so helper lanes stay alive to its end; OpDemoteToHelperInvocation is a `DISCARD` that flags the lane and goes on.
The wait before the result's first reader is on the instruction before it.
A vertex shader samples the same heap, its bindings' handles in its FAU block after its own words (the viewport's, and those of its built-ins), the driver giving its stage the resource table; it has no derivatives, so an implicit level is the base level (LOD mode none) and a bias is refused.

## Derivatives and helper invocations

A derivative is `CLPER.subgroup4` and a subtraction (valhall 13.10.2): the coarse one is lane 1's (or 2's) value less lane 0's, the fine one the neighbour's value less the lane's with its sign flipped in the odd lane by an exclusive-or of the lane id.
The shader has no `discard` flow, so a quad's helper lanes live to its end.
OpDemoteToHelperInvocation is `DISCARD`; a shader that asks whether it is a helper keeps a register that a demote sets, and the answer is a zero low half of the coverage register r61 or that register.

## Built-ins

A vertex shader's VertexIndex is r60 (it includes the first vertex), InstanceIndex is r61 plus the base instance, BaseVertex and BaseInstance are FAU words the driver fills (`GraphicsFauLayout.baseVertexWord`, `baseInstanceWord`, `block`'s `baseVertex` and `baseInstance`), and DrawIndex is r62, which holds a value only when the draw enables its draw-id register (`VertexShader.readsDrawIndex`).
A layer or a viewport index is a store of one byte at offset 2 of the position FIFO's attribute section (`LEA_BUF_IMM` at the vertex slot plus four, behind bit 1 of `shader_output`), and the shader says `VertexShader.extendedRecord`, so the draw's FIFO format is the extended one; both together are refused, as they share the field.
A fragment shader's SampleId is the five low bits of r61's second byte, SampleMask in is its low half, SamplePosition a load from the sample position table (the special entry `sample`, `FragmentShader.readsSamplePositions`) indexed by the sample id, PointCoord `LD_VAR_SPECIAL.v2 point`, PrimitiveId r57, Layer the low byte of the frame argument r62 (the driver writes the layer there), HelperInvocation as above.
FragDepth is `ZS_EMIT.z` and FragStencilRefEXT `ZS_EMIT.stencil` (one `ZS_EMIT` for both) after the `ATEST` and its wait, and a SampleMask written is ANDed into r60 before the `ATEST` (`FragmentShader.writesDepth`, `writesStencil`, `writesSampleMask`, `perSampleShading`); a vertex shader that writes Layer says so (`VertexShader.writesLayer`), for the draw's layer index and extended record.
With `BackendOptions.viewMask`, a vertex shader is compiled for a multiview draw (valhall 07 section 7.8.2, 12c.4.1): its blocks are replicated once for each view of the mask, one after the other, ViewIndex the copy's view, and each copy's position and varyings are stored at `r59` plus the view's bit position times 4 slots (8 with the extended record).
An integer colour output is a `BLEND` in the render target's own format (`auto32`), and the alpha test is skipped for it.
A compute shader's NumWorkgroups, WorkgroupSize, the ids and indices, SubgroupSize and the subgroup ids are as under the compute ABI above.
ClipDistance, CullDistance, barycentrics and a fragment shader's ViewportIndex are refused (14.3.7).

## The FAU layout

`FauLayout` says where the block holds what the shader reads, in this order:
one slot for the 64-bit address of each storage or uniform buffer the shader names, in ascending (set, binding) order;
the number of workgroups (x, y, z, pad), when read;
the byte size of each buffer whose run-time array length is read, one 32-bit word each;
the heap handle of element 0 of each storage image or texel buffer binding the shader names, one 32-bit word each, in ascending (set, binding) order (`imageBindings`; `block` takes `imageHandle`);
the push constants, word n at byte offset 4n, up to the highest word read.
`FauLayout` is plain data in MaliCore, so a dispatch needs no compiler to use it.
`FauLayout.block` builds the 64-bit words from the buffers' addresses and sizes, the push constants and the grid.
A shader that needs more than 128 slots is refused.

## Selection

i32 and f32 are one register, bool is 0 or all ones, i64 and global pointers are an even pair.
Buffer, workgroup and stack pointers are followed at compile time: the constant offset goes in the access's 16-bit field, and a run-time offset (with a power-of-two multiply folded in) is one `SHADD`.
A compare used once by a select in its block is one `CSEL`.
Integer division and remainder have no instruction (valhall 11b.1), so `expandIntegerDivision` (shadercompiler(2)) rewrites them before selection.
A 32-bit one is `U32_TO_F32`, `FRCP`, products, `F32_TO_U32`, `IMUL` and two correction steps of `ICMP` and `CSEL`: the reciprocal is taken a little low so the first quotient never exceeds the true one, one more estimate of the remainder gives a quotient at most one low, and a correction step settles it.
A 64-bit one takes that sequence when both operands are below 2^32 and otherwise a loop of 64 restoring steps; a constant 32-bit divisor is the multiply-high method.
The float estimate is exact whether `FRCP` errs low or high by an ulp.
A zero divisor gives the reference sequences' results (valhall 11b.8.1.6), INT_MIN / -1 gives INT_MIN.
Conversions between 64-bit integers and f32 are sequences of 32-bit operations, rounding to nearest even.
Float64 is refused by name: the hardware has none.
64-bit atomics (add, sub, and, or, xor, exchange, min, max and compare-exchange) are the `ATOM`, `ATOM_RETURN` and `ACMPXCHG` forms with 64-bit data.
`fdiv`, `fsqrt`, `fexp2`, `flog2`, `fsin` and `fcos` use the sequences of valhall 11c.13: `fsqrt` is `frcp` of `frsq`, exp2 and log2 within the spec's bounds, sine and cosine with pi/2 in two parts (the residual angle is a fused multiply-add of the quantised angle by each part), so they stay within 2^-14 up to about 411000 radians and 2^-15.7 on -pi to pi.
Sine and cosine are right to 2^-11 on -pi to pi (Vulkan's bound); beyond about 411000 the tables return negative zero and the result is wrong.
Blocks end in the branches of 13.4; a block before a join carries `reconverge`; a block drains its message slots before its exits, so every block starts with none busy.

## Narrow types

An 8 or 16-bit integer is an `i32` register whose upper bits are not defined (the compiler's `legalizeNarrowIntegers`): add, subtract, multiply, the bitwise operations, left shift, select and phi need no fix, and an operation that looks at the upper bits first widens its operand by the type's signedness with one conversion (`S8_TO_S32`, `U16_TO_U32`, ...): compares, min and max, division, right shifts, conversions to float and to wider integers.
Memory keeps the width: a load is `LOAD.i8` or `LOAD.i16` with the lane `w0` (the register's upper bits are not defined: the G720 sign extends it, 0x83 reads 0xffffff83), a store `STORE.i8` or `STORE.i16`, and a push constant is its word shifted down.
Division of 8 and 16-bit operands is the 32-bit sequence of the sign-extended or zero-extended operands.
A float16 value is a register whose low half is the binary16 value: `fadd`, `fsub`, `fmul`, `fma`, `fneg`, `fabs`, `fmin`, `fmax` and the comparisons are the `v2f16` forms of `FADD`, `FMA`, `FMIN`, `FMAX` and `FCMP` with both sources read as lane 0 (swizzle `h00`), so the result is correctly rounded binary16; conversions are `F16_TO_F32` and the narrowing destination of `FADD` (`F32_TO_F16`); a conversion between float16 and an integer goes through binary32.
Division, square root, reciprocal square root, exp2, log2, sine, cosine and rounding to an integer of float16 are computed in binary32 and rounded once (11c.13).
Two float16 values are not paired into one `v2f16` instruction: each operation computes both halves, and one result is used.

## Atomics

Integer atomics on 32 and 64-bit locations in buffers, workgroup memory and storage images are the `ATOM`, `ATOM_RETURN`, `ATOM1` and `ACMPXCHG` forms; a 64-bit atomic load or store is `LOAD.i64` or `STORE.i64`.
The instruction set has no float atomic and none of 8 or 16 bits (12a.8.8), so a float add, minimum or maximum (binary32 or binary16) and every 8 or 16-bit read-modify-write is a loop on the containing aligned 32-bit word: read the word, compute the new word (from the float value of the old one, or by replacing the lane inside the word), `ACMPXCHG`, and retry while the exchange returns something else than the word read.
The exchange compares bit patterns, so a negative zero and a positive zero are different words and a NaN equals itself.
A float exchange is `ATOM_RETURN` with `axchg` on the bits.
An atomic on a storage image chosen by a value that differs between lanes is done in the same loop over the distinct handles that texture accesses get, with the texel's address and the atomics on it inside it.
There is no scope or order field on an atomic, and all atomics are done at the shared L2, so the memory scope and semantics become only waits (12a.8.8.1, 12a.9): a relaxed atomic is the atomic alone; release, acquire-release and sequentially consistent semantics wait for every earlier store and atomic before the atomic issues; acquire, acquire-release and sequentially consistent wait for the atomic's own slot before anything that follows; the invocation scope orders nothing between invocations.
A workgroup barrier is the sequence of 12a.9 whatever the memory scope it names; an execution barrier at subgroup or invocation scope is no instruction, and only its memory semantics' waits remain.

## Subgroups

The subgroup size is 16, a warp, and subgroup operations are for compute and fragment shaders (a shader that uses one does not allow workgroup merging).
Every OpGroupNonUniform operation is built from `WMASK` (the ballot) and `CLPER` (the permute) as 13.9.3 says (shadercompiler(2)): a reduction or scan is a loop over the 16 lanes through constant-lane permutes so it is right with any set of lanes executing, and a bool, narrow or 64-bit value travels as 32-bit words.
Whether `WMASK` reports the lanes executing it or every lane launched is open (13.9.4), and the compiler asks one function for a ballot: by default one `WMASK` (the reference compiler's reading); with `BackendOptions.ballotWithoutWMASK` the operand's low bit is permuted in from each of the 16 lanes and shifted into place, which is right whichever `WMASK` does (a permute gives zero for a lane that is not executing), for about thirty more instructions a ballot.
The Valhall interpreter has both readings (`DispatchConfig.wmaskLaunched`), and the tests run the same shaders under both.
Every conditional branch and every predecessor of a join carries `reconverge` (13.5.2, 13.9.5), never the join block itself, and the copy block of a back edge is placed beside its latch, so lanes that leave a loop at different iterations meet at the exit and a barrier or a ballot after the loop sees the whole warp.

## Precision

Measured on the interpreter over dense sweeps against the correctly rounded result computed in double precision, in units in the last place of the result (the worst of 20000 points a function, half of them log-spaced), beside Vulkan's bound (the "Precision and Operation of SPIR-V Instructions" table; a function it says is inherited from a formula has that formula's bound derived):

| Function | Domain | Measured (ulp) | Bound |
| fdiv | any | 1.34 | 2.5 |
| sqrt | 1e-30 to 1e30 | 1.43 | 4.5 (inherited from 1/inversesqrt) |
| inversesqrt | 1e-30 to 1e30 | 0.50 | 2 |
| exp | -87 to 88 | 0.98 | 3 + 2 abs x |
| exp2 | -125 to 127 | 0.84 | 3 + 2 abs x |
| log | 1e-30 to 1e30 | 1.22 (abs 6.3e-6) | 3 outside 0.5 to 2, 2^-21 absolute inside |
| log2 | 1e-30 to 1e30 | 0.55 (abs 3.8e-6) | the same |
| pow | x 0.01 to 100, y -8 to 8 | 21.6 | derived from exp2(y log2 x), 3 + 2 abs z + the logarithm's error carried through |
| sin, cos | -pi to pi | abs 2.0e-5 | abs 2^-11 |
| sin, cos | up to 400000 | abs 4.4e-5 | none |
| tan | -1.5 to 1.5 | 5300 | derived from sin / cos |
| asin | -1 to 1 | 2377 | 4096 |
| acos | -1 to 1 | 760 | 4096 |
| atan | any | 347 | 4096 |
| atan2 | any | 347 | 4096 + 2.5 |
| sinh | -88 to 88 | 61 | derived from (exp x - exp -x) / 2 |
| cosh | -88 to 88 | 61 | derived from (exp x + exp -x) / 2 |
| tanh | -20 to 20 | 3.4 | derived from sinh / cosh |
| asinh | -1000 to 1000 | 6.3 | derived from log(x + sqrt(x^2 + 1)) |
| acosh | 1 to 1001 | 2.2 | derived from log(x + sqrt(x^2 - 1)) |
| atanh | -0.999 to 0.999 | 3.1 | derived from 0.5 log((1 + x) / (1 - x)) |

On binary16 every one of those functions is within 1 unit in the last place of binary16 (exhaustive-sample sweeps, 0.98 at worst, sine, cosine and tangent), since each is computed in binary32 and rounded once; the 4096 ulp functions are about 0.07 binary16 ulp.
Division within 2.5 holds for divisors from 2^-126 to 2^126; results below the smallest normal are not compared.
`exp` and `pow` carry the exponent product's rounding through (valhall 11c.13.6 gives the plain sequence), which is the difference between 0.98 and 61 ulp for `exp` near 46.

## Storage images

An `imgload`, `imgstore` or `imgaddr` is `LD_TEX` with the register format of the shader's result type (`f32`, `u32` or `s32`, never `auto32` for a load), `LEA_TEX` then `ST_CVT` with `auto32`, or `LEA_TEX` alone (valhall 12b.10, 12b.11).
A `descword` (the image queries, 12b.13.5) is `LD_PKA.i32` through the pseudo-table handle, with the null test and the fields in arithmetic.
The image's handle is the binding's FAU word plus the element index; it is one value for a warp (12b.2.1), so an index that may differ between lanes becomes the loop over the distinct values the texture handles use, and an atomic on an image chosen that way is refused.
The coordinates are packed in two words of 16-bit halves; the high halves of x and y are ORed into the first free coordinate, so a negative or beyond-16-bit coordinate is out of bounds.
A load reads all the format's channels in one `LD_TEX` and the shader reads them by position.
A store is `LEA_TEX` (the address's two words and the conversion word) and `ST_CVT` of the channels.
An atomic is `LEA_TEX` and the global atomic at the address.
The format of the access is the descriptor's: the shader says only how many channels the SPIR-V format has and their kind, so Rgba8 reads and writes a bgra8 image.
A fragment shader may use them; a vertex shader may not.

## Texel buffers

A texel buffer is a Buffer descriptor (type 9, buffer type 3) in the same resource table as the storage images and textures, named by a handle in the same way: the binding's FAU word plus the element of the binding's array, one value for a warp.
The element index within the buffer may differ between lanes.
An `imgload`, `imgstore` or `imgaddr` with `ImgFlags.buffer` (a fetch of a samplerBuffer, an imageBuffer's read, write and atomics) starts with `LEA_BUF` (the element index and the handle): the element's address.
The conversion word is word 7 of the descriptor, read by `LD_PKA.i32` through the pseudo-table at byte offset 32 * index + 28, and a word of zero is replaced by 0x5F0E7, the constant format that reads every channel as zero (valhall 12b.12, 12b.14.1).
A load is `LD_CVT` of the address and the conversion word, in the register format of the shader's result type and as many channels as it reads (four for a fetch of a samplerBuffer, whose format is the descriptor's); a store is `ST_CVT`, `auto32`, of every channel the shader writes, not trimmed to the format's; an atomic is the global atomic at the `LEA_BUF` address.
The size query is the descriptor's words 1 and 4, an unsigned divide.
A store or an atomic ends the sharing of a fetch's result with the later fetches of the same element in the block.
A store, or an atomic and the address it uses, is made only where the element is inside the buffer: `(element + 1) * stride <= size` in 64 bits with a stride that is not zero, from the descriptor's words 1 and 4 (an access past the end gives `LEA_BUF` an address that faults, valhall 12b.10.5), and an atomic that has a result gives 0 past the end.
A load is not guarded: it reads what is there past the end.

## Registers

Linear scan over one interval for each virtual register, in class 32 first and then class 64.
A shader that does not fit class 64 spills: each write of the chosen value stores, each read fills, at 8-byte slots after the shader's own stack objects, and `stackBytes` covers them.

