# BUGS

Vertex and fragment shaders have no thread storage in the draw, so a spill or a stack variable gives a non-zero `stackBytes` the driver must honour; a vertex shader that is not divided (control flow, multiview, a NoPerspective varying) runs whole in both IDVS passes, so its varyings' work is also done in the position pass; derivatives other than an implicit level, FragDepth and integer colour outputs, point size and layer outputs are refused or not stored; the attribute loads and the varying and special loads are checked by the validator and, for the vertex stage's stores, on the interpreter (whose model of the hardware vertex buffer is a test's), not yet on a GPU except the position record.
Double floats, cube, depth and multisampled storage images, 3D and unknown-format storage images, derivatives in a compute shader, pointers in phis or selects and push constants read at a run-time offset are refused (`unsupported`).
Sine and cosine are wrong past about 411000 radians; beyond 1100 they are good to about 4e-5, not the 2^-15.7 of -pi to pi.
The subgroup size is 16 and the lanes of a subgroup are the consecutive local invocation indices; vertex shaders have no subgroup operations; scopes other than Subgroup, group operations other than Reduce, InclusiveScan, ExclusiveScan and ClusteredReduce, and an execution barrier in divergent code are not handled.
On the G720 WMASK of a constant reports the lanes active now (0x00ff for eight of sixteen lanes, 0xaaaa in a branch the odd lanes take, whether the others skip to a reconverge or end), as the subgroup operations assume; what an inactive CLPER source gives beyond the constant and what a lane discarded but not retired does to a subgroup operation are not measured (13.9.1, 13.9.4).
A compare-exchange loop is bounded at 2^20 rounds, since no forward progress between warps is stated (12a.8.8.2): past that it ends without having applied its update and the atomic returns the last word it read, as if another warp had written last.
The float and 8 and 16-bit atomic loops and the 64-bit atomic load and store ran on the interpreter, and on the G720 the loops are exact under contention in memory the GPU maps cacheable: 16384 lanes of 256 workgroups each adding 1 to one counter finished with the counter exact for a float add (31 ms for the dispatch, a hardware add taking 2.6 ms) and a 16-bit add (32 ms), with the neighbouring half untouched; an atomic of any kind in memory mapped write-combining raises GPU_CACHEABILITY_FAULT and does nothing, so a driver gives atomic memory the cacheable mapping (mali(3)).
A float16 value in a register is half of a 32-bit register, so a shader that is bound by float16 arithmetic does twice the instructions it could; the 16-bit register formats of `LD_TEX` and `ST_CVT` are not modelled by the interpreter.
A subgroup reduction or scan is a loop over the 16 lanes, 16 permutes and as many operations, instead of the log2 16 steps of a doubling scan, which are wrong when a lane in the middle is not executing.
The sequences for sinh, cosh, asinh, acosh and atanh and the 4096-ulp inverse trigonometric functions are built from the exponential, logarithm and polynomials of 11c.13.12 and have been measured on the interpreter only; asin has 2377 ulp of error just above its series cut-off, and tan loses precision near the poles as sin / cos does.
A 64-bit division whose operands do not both fit 32 bits runs 64 steps; the 32-bit sequence assumes the reciprocal is within a unit in the last place (a second correction step covers a little more).
Storage image accesses have been run on the interpreter over a test model of the images (linear, the formats rgba8, bgra8, r8, rgba8ui, r32ui, r32i, r32f, rgba16f, rgba32f and rgba32ui) and checked against the encodings and the validator, not on a GPU; what an access outside the image reads, what a conversion does to an out-of-range integer, the image's layout beyond linear and an image size query are the spec's open questions (12b.10, 12b.13.2).
Texel-buffer accesses have been run on the interpreter over a model of the Buffer descriptors and of the pixel formats (the numeric families, RGB10 A2 and the constant format; not sRGB), and on the Orange Pi 6 Plus: fetches of r32float, rgba8, rg32float and r8 views as four channels are exact, and `LD_CVT` does fill the channels a format lacks with the component order's constants, (R, 0, 0, 1) and (R, G, 0, 1), so the backend does no software fill; a store and the read after it, in program order, are right.
Atomics did nothing on the board while the GPU's mappings were write-combining (an atomic there raises GPU_CACHEABILITY_FAULT and changes nothing); in GPU-cacheable memory they are exact (gpu-test's contended counters), and the reference maps all ordinary memory cacheable (panthor 04 §4.7.3).
A block's slots are drained at its end, so a store before a join costs a wait there even where the join's code would not need it.
A wait in an instruction's flow field takes effect after that instruction, so the backend puts a wait on the instruction before its consumer (or on a NOP when that one issues a message or already carries a flow value), never on the consumer itself; the interpreter faults on a read the carrier's own wait would have covered.
The last-use marks skip registers a message still reads.
The interpreter's texture unit is a model (filters, address modes, levels from the quad's derivatives, gather, compare, cube in the direction form), not the hardware: the level of detail is the isotropic formula, the sampler's bias and limits apply to every level mode that reads a level (12b.5.5 says so for the computed ones), the level word's minimum combines with the sampler's as a maximum, a depth compare gives its result in channel 0 and (0, 0, 1) in the others, a fetch outside its level reads zeros, a cube filter does not cross faces and the face numbering and signs are the graphics APIs', and a quad whose neighbour is not running the instruction gives a zero difference; the spec leaves each open (12b.5.3, 12b.5.5, 12b.6, 12b.7), so a sample's exact values are the board's to confirm, and the tests compare the model with itself and check the compiler's staging.
Whether an explicit level applies the sampler's bias and limits, how a cube's face-selecting direction form numbers the faces, what the gradient descriptor holds beyond the level, and the other open questions of 12b are for the board.
The wide form of the handle, the flag in the ISA's register type field and the helper lanes' retiring are the board's to confirm.
The heap handles are computed by the shader with 32-bit adds, so an index beyond the heap's size is the hardware's to refuse, and a combined image sampler is refused.
